description:
Added by the human on 2026-10-31 as the rollout's last task: the activation step that every CQ row scoped out.

Why it is needed. The pieces are built but nothing switches them on:
- CQ-R built the VM agent path: scripts/local-runner/agent-lifecycle.mjs (host side of one agent job), guest-session/guest-bridge, the guest-supervisor bridge mode and the R10d4 headless launch spec. Nothing calls agent-lifecycle.mjs except its tests and certify.
- The JetBrains Agents menu and scripts/moe-agent.{sh,ps1} always run the agent CLI on the host. The only VM UI is the Managed VM panel (status/reconcile/cleanup).
- assertExecutionProfileReady (packages/moe-daemon/src/delivery/executionProfile.ts:108) refuses verified-local unconditionally.
- CQ-M06a records non-promotion. Without this row, the rollout ends with everything built and certified but still switched off.

Objective. A project sets executionProfile verified-local, and readiness is proven from evidence. From then on, starting a worker or QA agent runs its CLI inside an owned Moe VM through agent-lifecycle.mjs:
- source goes in by the snapshot transfer;
- changes come back only through export and host admission;
- the host wrapper stays the only Git writer and lander;
- required checks run in their own check VMs (CQ-V, CQ-R11).
Architect and governor seats stay on the host unless the approved design says otherwise. JetBrains first; VS Code parity before the docs call it supported.

Size. This is larger than one task. Split it with moe-epic-breakdown into the daemon readiness gate, the JetBrains launch route, the wrapper route and VS Code parity, and keep this row as the final integration task.

Open decisions go to the human before submit_plan:
- one VM per job or per seat;
- a concurrency cap, since each VM is 2 CPUs, 4 GB RAM and 20 GB disk;
- what WSL agent mode does under verified-local;
- how the daemon trusts a certification report.

implementationPlan.description:
Composed lifecycle acceptance test. This is the epic's only cross-slice daemon test: each M07 slice tested its own seam, and this one walks one verified-local task through the real MCP registry in the exact order the VM-route wrappers call it after CQ-M07r/s/t. Read scripts/moe-agent.sh's VM route for that order: seat-start, marked claim, seat-job, land_vm_candidate, then wt_land_rounds with the check-VM gate, the receipt and finalize.

New file packages/moe-daemon/src/__acceptance__/verifiedLocalActivation.acceptance.test.ts.

SET-UP
- ToolTestHarness plus createMcpCaller from __acceptance__/mcpCall.ts. `call` is /mcp. `runnerCall` carries authenticateRunner's identity from a temp host key. Copy the callers() set-up of deliveryBypass.acceptance.test.ts; never import from that test file.
- Hold readiness READY without mocking: `refreshReadiness(inputs, async () => READY)`, as readinessHolder.test.ts:35 `hold` does, with clearReadiness() in afterEach. Never vi.spyOn executionProfile (deliveryBypass does): this test must exercise the real assertExecutionProfileReady and activationGaps.
- Settings: deliveryPolicy 'local-branch', worktreeMode 'on', qualityGate GATE, checkEnvironments [ENV] (ENV = 'moe-local-vm-v1:' + 64 hex). Then activate executionProfile 'verified-local' through h.state.updateSettings, the recorded path, and assert the PROJECT_SETTINGS_UPDATED record (transition 'activate', accepted).
- Error handling: assert each refusal by its exact codeName and that it wrote nothing (task bytes unchanged). A fixture or harness failure fails the test; it is never skipped or swallowed.

ROWS. Assert exact codeName and status with toBe/toStrictEqual, never truthiness. Read every code from source before asserting it (explore-before-assume).
- L1, the happy path:
  - submit_plan with executionContract.requiredChecks [{id:'gate', command: GATE, configs:['package-lock.json']}] (seed that file if the snapshot measure needs it); task.executionPolicySnapshot is verified-local;
  - worker claim_next_task without executionRoute is NOT_ALLOWED EXECUTION_ROUTE_REQUIRED; with executionRoute 'vm' it is claimed;
  - get_context with sessionId; start_step, complete_step, then complete_task with verification, which moves the task to REVIEW;
  - the wrapper's record_candidate (tree M on base B) and its record_commit completion row;
  - runnerCall record_check_dispatch, then record_check_observation (passed, exitCode 0, ENV) on that candidate;
  - finalize_attempt landed, then record_delivery_receipt (target, targetBefore B, targetAfter C, landedRevision C);
  - the QA claim of the REVIEW row is refused without the route and claimed with it;
  - QA get_context with sessionId, then qa_approve with the stamped identity and candidateId: the task is DONE.
- L2, the consumer: a second verified-local task with dependsOn [the first] is claimable (with the route) once the first is DONE. After archive_task of the first it stays claimable through the receipt. A twin with no receipt is withheld, naming delivery-receipt.
- L3, one defect each, A0-style:
  - (a) no observation: qa_approve is DELIVERY_EVIDENCE_MISSING, naming the required-check token;
  - (b) only a /mcp record_check_run (what the old host L6 sent): still refused;
  - (c) a dispatch in an environment checkEnvironments does not list: CHECK_ENVIRONMENT_NOT_APPROVED, and nothing is written;
  - (d) after clearReadiness(), the next strict claim is EXECUTION_PROFILE_NOT_READY.

MUTATION CHECK, on a scratch copy, reverted after: drop the readiness hold, and L1 must fail at the settings update; drop the receipt, and L2's archived case must fail. Record both in the step note.

Run checkRefs[2] and quote its counts.

comments.content:
@architect-b742aab1 Scope extension from the human (2026-10-31, "make sure that our board in the end will have complete system"). This row is now the board's final whole-system acceptance, not only the activation.

Dependencies. dependsOn now covers every open row on the board, transitively:
- M06 (ced85748), which covers I06, V12 and M06a;
- R13 (f4811a72);
- CQ-RF07 (f3f364a6);
- WT-19 (b1ca54f2), which covers the worktree epic and the Stability bugs;
- the three certify fixes that R13's 20-id cap could not hold: 4679ec68, 47d21b83, 77b0637f.
Any new row filed from here on should be wired under one of these, or under this row directly.

On the create_task warning: it flagged this row as a second hardening row next to M06. Keep both. M06 is integration before activation and records non-promotion. This row comes after activation and is the last acceptance.

Extra DoD items for this row, on top of its description:
1. After activation and with worktreeMode on (WT-19), the full regression passes, run in a git-archive snapshot, never in place:
   - daemon, proxy and claude-plugin npm test;
   - JetBrains ./gradlew test and buildPlugin;
   - VS Code npm test and npm run compile;
   - bash scripts/tests/postflight.sh (plus the .ps1 sibling), scripts/tests/parity-check.sh, and npm run lint.
2. A short end-to-end on the real board in both legacy and verified-local, run by the human or the governor: architect plan → worker → QA → landed DONE. The outcome is recorded as a comment here.
3. The plugin built from that tree is installed, and the CQ-M06a record is updated with the promotion decision and the per-platform status. macOS stays "not certified" until the human has a Mac.

Ask the human the open decisions (VM per job or per seat, the concurrency cap, WSL agent mode, report trust) before submit_plan. Split the slices with moe-epic-breakdown and keep this row last.

ADVISORY from the CQ-M07a QA (qa-97c4310b, approved 2026-11-01). Non-blocking; fold it in only if this row touches certificationEvidence.test.ts anyway.
- The drift table in certificationEvidence.test.ts catches every clause-level mutant of the checkCertificationReport port: 95 of 112 QA mutants killed, plus 15 of 15 reader mutants.
- 6 strict-to-loose equality swaps (=== to ==) still survive: report.kind, version, transport, provider.name, executionReady and the check-row id. certify.mjs mirrors 3 of them.
- The shipped port is correct: it refuses these inputs exactly as certify.mjs does. The gap is only that a future == regression would go unseen.
- To kill them, add refused VARIANTS rows with JSON type-confusion values:
  - version '1' or true;
  - kind ['isolation-certification'];
  - transport ['injected'] with certified false;
  - provider name ['multipass'];
  - executionReady 0;
  - one check id as ['provision'].
- The file sits at the 400-line cap for its slice: fold the rows 2-3 per line.

QA follow-up from CQ-M07g (task-9076afab, approved, completion 493ea8a4). This is a test gap, not a defect. No row pins the production default of the seat runner probe in scripts/local-runner/vm-control.mjs. Every VC-C3/VC-R2 row injects runnerAlive, so three mutants pass all tests:
- cleanupReport `runnerAlive = runnerProbe` changed to `() => false`;
- the same change in reconcileReport;
- runVmControl `deps.runnerAlive ?? (() => false)`.
Any of them would purge a seat VM whose runner is still alive on this host.

Suggested row for whichever slice next edits local-runner-vm-control.test.mjs (or the M07 integration):
- one seat record with runner `${process.pid}@x`, host localRunnerHost() and platform process.platform;
- the fake daemon says gone, and the lease is free;
- call cleanupReport and reconcileReport (or runVmControl with deps.call only), passing NO runnerAlive;
- expect preserved/held reason 'runner-alive' and zero provider delete calls.

The QA harness for the check: /tmp/qa-m07g-mut/run.mjs (mutants M28-M30).
